WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.607 --> 00:00:08.574
Good morning, my name is Rob Phillips

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:11.978
and I'm from Caltech. Today, I'm at Woods Hole in

00:00:11.979 --> 00:00:15.648
Massachusetts. Super privileged to be here. And

00:00:15.649 --> 00:00:17.683
I'm going to be talking about great questions in

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:21.587
the life sciences. And these are questions where it's

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:24.223
thought that maybe physics and computation will be able

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:28.094
to shed some interesting light. Now the question I'm posing

00:00:28.095 --> 00:00:32.765
today is, can we find a way to do what I would call,

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:37.270
decipher the modern Rosetta Stone? Here are just

00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:40.473
to give you a sense, a historically inaccurate sense,

00:00:40.474 --> 00:00:44.744
of the rosetta stone and its history. The basic story goes

00:00:44.745 --> 00:00:48.748
something like this. Napoleon had grand ambitions, he decided

00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:52.084
to go to Egypt, and when he got there, his soldiers they

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:55.254
ransacked the place. And one of the things that happened is

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:59.859
they found this rock, the Rosetta Stone. It led to a variety

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.763
of geniuses, such as Thomas Young, known for his study of

00:01:03.764 --> 00:01:08.301
interference of light. And Champollion to decipher

00:01:08.302 --> 00:01:12.138
the Rosetta Stone. Of course, from a historical, from an

00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:14.574
archeological perspective, this is clearly a very interesting

00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:18.444
thing. But, I would say that at the moment, we're living through

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:21.814
an era where there are much bigger fish to fry. There's

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.684
much more sort of demanding set of questions of

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:27.153
deciphering something. And that's that we're living in

00:01:27.154 --> 00:01:30.456
the genomic era. So, the question that I'm going to pose

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:34.760
today has to do with, okay, we can now more or less

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:37.930
at will, sequence genomes. We can sequence genomes

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:41.033
of human beings and a generic organism that we discover.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:45.104
Bacteria, whatever. What this leads to is long strings

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:49.141
of letters, A's, T's, G's, and C's. But it does leave us

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:52.712
wondering, what does it all mean? Now the thing I'm

00:01:52.713 --> 00:01:55.948
try to do in this talk is to give a sense of the extent to which

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.951
we successfully had a translation, like that that I showed you

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:03.990
here. Which is, it is a sort of translation table for the Rosetta

00:02:03.991 --> 00:02:07.793
Stone. And the extent to which there are gaping holes

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:11.063
in our understanding of this information content of genomes.

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:15.735
So, to go back to the 1960s, I want to tell you a little bit

00:02:15.736 --> 00:02:18.504
about what I think is one of the greatest experiments that was

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:21.741
ever done. In any science. This was done by Matthaei

00:02:21.742 --> 00:02:24.577
and Nirenberg, and it has to do with cracking the genetic code.

00:02:24.578 --> 00:02:28.881
The idea is that after the elucidation of the structure of

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:33.753
DNA by Watson and Crick, so 1953, there was a ten year

00:02:33.754 --> 00:02:37.190
rush, 15 year rush, to figure out what does the sequence

00:02:37.191 --> 00:02:41.494
of letters A, T, C, and G signify? And the reason that this is

00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:44.664
as I would say interesting in a certain puzzle is that we

00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:49.602
know that there are four nucleotides, A, T, G, and C, as I

00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:52.805
already mentioned. And we also know that a typical

00:02:52.806 --> 00:02:57.443
protein has on the order of 300 amino acids. Now if it takes

00:02:57.444 --> 00:03:01.147
3 nucleotides to code for each amino acid, then that means

00:03:01.148 --> 00:03:03.549
a typical sequence, a nucleotide sequence, is around

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:08.621
4^1000 different possibilities. It's a huge combinatorial

00:03:08.622 --> 00:03:11.524
space. And the question they set themselves was,

00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:14.894
how do we tame that huge complexity and beat it down

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:17.897
into a form where we would actually have some sort of

00:03:17.898 --> 00:03:20.366
translation table? The way this experiment happened

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.936
was synthetic biology, if you like, it wasn't called that

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:27.340
at the time. But what they did was they synthesized mRNAs

00:03:27.341 --> 00:03:32.845
that were repetitive, U-U-U-U-U and so on. And then they

00:03:32.846 --> 00:03:37.016
fed these mRNAs to a cell extract and asked the question,

00:03:37.017 --> 00:03:41.754
which of the radiolabeled amino acids are incorporated in

00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:43.923
the protein? And what they found is a chain,

00:03:43.924 --> 00:03:46.425
phenylalanine-phenylalanine-phenylalanine-phenylalanine

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:50.429
and so on. And the consequence was that they cracked the

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.933
genetic code. By doing this repeatedly, they were able to

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:57.937
essentially figure out the entire genetic code. So a way to

00:03:57.938 --> 00:04:00.873
think of this, most people have seen this before, you start

00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:03.809
from the inner circle, that tells you the first codon, the first

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.512
letter, then you go to the next circle out, that tells you the

00:04:06.513 --> 00:04:09.348
second one, the third circle out tells you the third,

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.451
and if you look at that triplet and then that will tell you

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:16.722
on the fourth ring, which amino acid goes with that collection

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:20.960
of nucleotides. The reason I say in the title that this is only a

00:04:20.961 --> 00:04:25.198
partial vocabulary, is that it is in a certain sense deceptive.

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:28.634
It makes it sound as though we've cracked this Rosetta Stone,

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:32.638
when in fact, we really haven't. The reason I say that is

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:36.075
illustrated I think, even in the case of the supposedly best

00:04:36.076 --> 00:04:39.212
understood organism, which is the bacterium, E. coli.

00:04:39.213 --> 00:04:42.849
If you go look at the databases of what we know about

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:47.253
E. coli, what you'll find is that 2000 of the roughly 4500

00:04:47.254 --> 00:04:51.457
genes have no regulatory annotation. Now what do I mean

00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:55.228
by that? What I mean is that, we indeed know where genes

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:59.031
start. We indeed know that certain amino acids will be

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:03.536
reduced and strung together as a result of that gene. But what we

00:05:03.537 --> 00:05:07.773
know nothing about is the decoration of the DNA in front of that

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.743
gene that tells it whether it should be on or not, in the first place.

00:05:10.744 --> 00:05:13.045
In other words, it's regulated. And we really don't know how

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:17.517
that works. In the case of eukaryotic cells, animal cells,

00:05:17.518 --> 00:05:20.286
the situation is even worse, as shown by the lower diagram

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:24.957
here. What that is, is it's showing a hunk of genomic DNA

00:05:24.958 --> 00:05:29.228
and this green part is known as an enhancer. Enhancer refers

00:05:29.229 --> 00:05:33.666
to a little chunk of DNA that can be as far away as megabases

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:37.737
from the gene it controls. And then it bends over and kisses

00:05:37.738 --> 00:05:42.175
its regulatory region onto the basal transcription apparatus

00:05:42.176 --> 00:05:45.978
and turns the gene on. The problem is, if I handed you some

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:48.414
genomic sequence and said, okay, you tell me where the

00:05:48.415 --> 00:05:51.317
enhancers are and you tell me which genes they interact

00:05:51.318 --> 00:05:56.022
with, it would be very hard at best. Okay, so I claim that

00:05:56.023 --> 00:05:58.391
this means we really haven't cracked the full genetic

00:05:58.392 --> 00:06:02.228
code. And so, this is to me really the great question of

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:05.731
this particular talk. But I want to tell you a little bit about

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:08.100
approaches that have been taken. This is from an experiment

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:12.071
that was done by Justin Kinney and his PhD thesis work, which I

00:06:12.072 --> 00:06:16.509
think is really quite cool as a way of looking forward to

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.579
own the genome with single base-pair resolution. So I show

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:23.850
in this diagram, I have some promoter, some region of DNA

00:06:23.851 --> 00:06:26.452
that controls the gene that I'm interested in. I want to figure

00:06:26.453 --> 00:06:28.654
-- and I don't know what the regulatory behavior is,

00:06:28.655 --> 00:06:31.591
I want to find that out. So how do we do that? His idea was,

00:06:31.592 --> 00:06:36.662
let's take the DNA and mutate it at about 10%, in other words,

00:06:36.663 --> 00:06:39.799
10 out of every 100 bases will screw up. We'll change them

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:43.436
from A to T, and A to G, and so on. And then we'll ask the

00:06:43.437 --> 00:06:49.242
question, if we wire up cells, each cell having a different version

00:06:49.243 --> 00:06:52.812
of this mutagenized promoter, how much fluorescence does this

00:06:52.813 --> 00:06:55.114
cell produce? The fluorescence is used as a way to read

00:06:55.115 --> 00:06:59.819
out how much we've messed up the gene. And so as shown in this

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.755
diagram, he makes a library of what are called plasmids, these

00:07:02.756 --> 00:07:06.125
are small circular DNA with his gene of interest. Mutagenized

00:07:06.126 --> 00:07:09.862
gene, the cells are fluorescent. Then what he does is

00:07:09.863 --> 00:07:13.065
sorts the cells according to how much fluorescence they have, that's

00:07:13.066 --> 00:07:17.103
what you see here, the sorting scheme. And then categorizes

00:07:17.104 --> 00:07:20.173
them according to how high or low their fluorescence is

00:07:20.174 --> 00:07:22.975
and that's the point of this bottom diagram. The group called

00:07:22.976 --> 00:07:27.680
B1, B2, B3, and so on, those are bins in which the cells

00:07:27.681 --> 00:07:30.883
have different levels of fluorescence. And each collection

00:07:30.884 --> 00:07:33.853
has a different fluorescence. So that's the key idea.

00:07:33.854 --> 00:07:38.558
For me, where physics and sort of theory meets this kind of

00:07:38.559 --> 00:07:41.494
experiment is this graph shown here in the upper right.

00:07:41.495 --> 00:07:44.564
Which tells us the relationship between sequence and

00:07:44.565 --> 00:07:47.934
which bin a given cell got sorted into. Let me try and give you

00:07:47.935 --> 00:07:52.405
an analogy. I'm in a room right now, there's a door, if someone

00:07:52.406 --> 00:07:54.740
were to walk in the door right now, I could make a guess

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:57.176
about the height of that person. How might I do that? Well

00:07:57.177 --> 00:07:59.812
I would just reason, well, typical human beings are between

00:07:59.813 --> 00:08:02.849
5' and 6' feet tall, I would do something along those lines.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.085
If somebody told me the next person to walk in is going to be

00:08:06.086 --> 00:08:09.522
a woman, then I would do much better on localizing that person's

00:08:09.523 --> 00:08:13.092
height. Now why is that? It's because there's what's called a

00:08:13.093 --> 00:08:17.830
mutual information between height and gender. Just like in this

00:08:17.831 --> 00:08:21.667
case, there's a mutual information between which base,

00:08:21.668 --> 00:08:27.006
is it an A, a T, a G, or a C, at a given site. And which bin

00:08:27.007 --> 00:08:30.610
the cells will be sorted into. So, what comes out of this

00:08:30.611 --> 00:08:34.647
is what's called an information footprint. Ideas that tells

00:08:34.648 --> 00:08:37.383
you what bases, at least the ones labeled in orange,

00:08:37.384 --> 00:08:40.520
which bases are the ones that are important for gene expression.

00:08:40.521 --> 00:08:44.223
And so, what you can do with this method is, you can take -- go to

00:08:44.224 --> 00:08:47.827
a region of the genome which you're ignorant about, and you can

00:08:47.828 --> 00:08:52.298
figure out where transcription factors will bind. And you can start

00:08:52.299 --> 00:08:56.302
to decode this next layer of information that's hidden in the genome.

00:08:56.303 --> 00:09:01.140
So, let's think about this. What I've told you is that there's a challenge,

00:09:01.141 --> 00:09:05.244
first of all, we really don't understand the genome in detail at the

00:09:05.245 --> 00:09:09.182
-- at all levels of information. But let's say we solve the problem

00:09:09.183 --> 00:09:12.385
in the -- using the type of scheme that I just talked about.

00:09:12.386 --> 00:09:15.988
Then what I would say is, we have a translation table

00:09:15.989 --> 00:09:19.659
like this picture that I showed you earlier, which comes from Champollion,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.695
which was basically telling us the meaning of the symbols

00:09:22.696 --> 00:09:27.133
in the hieroglyphics. But what I want you to consider is, having

00:09:27.134 --> 00:09:30.369
the list of symbols, if I knew the letters in the English alphabet,

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.172
and even perhaps, if I knew the meaning of, if I had words in

00:09:33.173 --> 00:09:35.942
the English alphabet, that doesn't imply that I'm ready to write

00:09:35.943 --> 00:09:39.278
a novel. So the vision that I really want to finish with is,

00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:41.781
let's say that one solves this problem of figuring out what the

00:09:41.782 --> 00:09:45.484
genome says at the level of the words, how do we compose

00:09:45.485 --> 00:09:48.321
a genomic novel? That's the question that I want to think about.

00:09:48.322 --> 00:09:52.792
So, in order to argue this, I want to go back in a way to

00:09:52.793 --> 00:09:55.494
engineering in the physics setting. I think of engineering

00:09:55.495 --> 00:09:58.664
as synthetic physics, and the reason I put the word "synthetic" in there

00:09:58.665 --> 00:10:02.635
is because I want to conjure up images of synthetic biology.

00:10:02.636 --> 00:10:06.906
So, engineering is synthetic physics. And often what one

00:10:06.907 --> 00:10:10.977
does is the simple problem first, a toy problem. You don't work on

00:10:10.978 --> 00:10:15.181
the airplane wing first, you work on flow past a sphere or flow past

00:10:15.182 --> 00:10:18.951
a cylinder, and you work out the rules of that problem and then

00:10:18.952 --> 00:10:22.421
once you have those rules in hand, then you turn to the more

00:10:22.422 --> 00:10:25.691
complicated situation. So the beauty of the picture I'm showing

00:10:25.692 --> 00:10:28.861
above is, this is the kind of thing that Leonardo Da Vinci was already

00:10:28.862 --> 00:10:32.999
thinking about. He was curious about flow past a sphere, he was able

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:37.003
to draw the vortices that you see in the picture. And my point is that

00:10:37.004 --> 00:10:40.806
after 150 years of fluid mechanics, we started understanding

00:10:40.807 --> 00:10:45.011
things well enough to then say, let's try to use these principles

00:10:45.012 --> 00:10:48.247
for something more tricky, which is for example, the shape of

00:10:48.248 --> 00:10:52.185
an airfoil. I love this picture, which shows, it's a Boeing picture,

00:10:52.186 --> 00:10:56.522
which shows a 747 and a 787. Really gives you a sense of

00:10:56.523 --> 00:11:01.327
where we've come with engineering. And the dream I guess

00:11:01.328 --> 00:11:04.530
I would like to articulate for biology is to have a biological

00:11:04.531 --> 00:11:08.000
engineering that bears the same relationship to fundamental

00:11:08.001 --> 00:11:12.004
science that this kind of thing in mechanical and electrical

00:11:12.005 --> 00:11:18.711
engineering do to physics. So, in a certain sense, what I would like

00:11:18.712 --> 00:11:22.181
is I'd like to be able to write the novel of the anglerfish. So the idea

00:11:22.182 --> 00:11:26.219
is, I could go grab a fish, sequence its genome, be able to

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:30.957
put the sequence into a computer to read it and say, "Okay, those

00:11:30.958 --> 00:11:34.961
are the genes, these are the regulatory regions, these over here

00:11:34.962 --> 00:11:40.666
are, in a certain sense, DNA fossils. And now I will reprogram

00:11:40.667 --> 00:11:44.470
this fish so that the color of the light that comes out of its little

00:11:44.471 --> 00:11:49.008
thing on its head is different." That's a vision for synthetic biology

00:11:49.009 --> 00:11:54.247
that would really be based on the infrastructure in deciphering

00:11:54.248 --> 00:11:58.117
the genomic Rosetta Stone. In summary, what I've tried to say,

00:11:58.118 --> 00:12:02.188
I think is a great question of our time, is the search to decipher

00:12:02.189 --> 00:12:05.091
these genomic Rosetta Stones. We've already learned a lot,

00:12:05.092 --> 00:12:08.628
but I think that in a certain sense, Thomas Paine once said,

00:12:08.629 --> 00:12:12.131
a long habit of thinking a thing wrong gives it a superficial

00:12:12.132 --> 00:12:15.434
appearance of being right. A long habit of thinking that we

00:12:15.435 --> 00:12:19.639
understand genomes and we've cracked the code gives it a

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.874
superficial appearance of having been solved, but I think

00:12:21.875 --> 00:12:25.778
really, it hasn't. And so, to me that's one of the great questions.

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:31.117
So I close by just saying it's a subjective matter, this issue of what are

00:12:31.118 --> 00:12:34.520
great questions. But that's the privilege of doing science, is that we get to

00:12:34.521 --> 00:12:38.558
ask questions that interest us and we're able to go pursue them.

00:12:38.559 --> 00:12:39.693
Thanks for listening.

